  "_commit": {
    "id": "155d3c55be29e50f306ccb73797d977c4d73ebe2",
    "author": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "committer": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "message": "Fix sf predicate/measure methods applying to every column, not just geometry\n\nst_intersects(), st_buffer(), st_area(), and ~50 other sf-compatible\nDuckDBTable methods built their SQL expression against every column of\nx via sql_call(), not just the geometry one. Calling e.g.\nst_intersects(a_duckdb_dataframe, y) on a layer with ordinary columns\nalongside \"geometry\" -- the routine shape for a DuckDBDataFrame used as\nan sf-like layer -- failed with a confusing DuckDB binder error instead\nof operating on the geometry column.\n\nAdd st_geometry.DuckDBTable, which narrows x to its one GEOMETRY-typed\ncolumn, detected by type rather than by name, erroring clearly if x\ndoes not have exactly one such column. Every affected method now\nnarrows through it via a shared .sql_call_geom() helper before building\nits SQL expression. Detecting by type rather than name also means\nDuckDBColumn's own delegation to these methods needs no changes: its\ninternal one-column table keeps the original column's name, not always\nliterally \"geometry\", and type-based detection doesn't care what that\nname is.\n",
    "time": 1794322905
  },
